        "content": "Weather reports (el parte meteorologico / el tiempo) and news bulletins (las noticias) appear frequently in the listening exam. For weather, you must know the key vocabulary thoroughly: hace sol (it's sunny), hace calor (it's hot), hace frio (it's cold), hace viento (it's windy), hace buen/mal tiempo (the weather is good/bad), llueve / esta lloviendo (it's raining), nieva / esta nevando (it's snowing), esta nublado (it's cloudy), hay niebla (it's foggy), hay tormenta (there's a storm), hay heladas (there's frost), las temperaturas (temperatures), los grados (degrees), maxima (maximum), minima (minimum), la prevision (the forecast), manana (tomorrow), esta tarde (this afternoon), esta noche (tonight), el fin de semana (the weekend). Geographic terms for Spain are important: el norte (north), el sur (south), el este (east), el oeste (west), la costa (coast), el interior (inland), las islas (islands), la peninsula (peninsula), Canarias, Baleares, Andalucia, Galicia, Cataluna, Castilla. For news bulletins, vocabulary includes: un accidente (accident), un incendio (fire), una huelga (strike), una manifestacion (demonstration/protest), un terremoto (earthquake), una inundacion (flood), un robo (robbery), detener (to arrest/detain), herido (injured), muerto (dead), los bomberos (firefighters), la policia (police), los servicios de emergencia (emergency services), el gobierno (government), el presidente (president), un portavoz (spokesperson), segun fuentes (according to sources). News items are typically short and factual, so focus on the 5 Ws: what happened, who was involved, where, when, and why. Numbers and statistics are common in news reports (casualties, monetary figures, percentages). Listen for time references that place the event: esta manana, ayer por la noche, el lunes pasado, hace dos horas.",
