        "title": "Sample Answers & Preparation Strategies",
        "content": "The most effective preparation strategy for prescribed literature is to create adaptable 'building blocks' that can be rearranged to answer different question types.\n\nBuilding Block 1 - Character descriptions: Write 5-6 sentences about each main character covering appearance, personality, motivations, and development. Memorise these. They can be used for character questions, but also inserted into theme answers when you need to discuss how a character embodies a theme.\n\nBuilding Block 2 - Key scene summaries: Choose 3-4 pivotal scenes from the text. For each, write 3-4 sentences describing what happens and why it matters. These scenes should illustrate different themes so you can deploy them flexibly. A good key scene includes conflict, emotion, and a turning point.\n\nBuilding Block 3 - Theme paragraphs: Write a paragraph (5-6 sentences) on each major theme. Include how the theme is introduced, developed, and resolved. Link each theme to at least one key scene and one character.\n\nBuilding Block 4 - Personal response: Write a genuine personal response (4-5 sentences) about what moved, surprised, challenged, or interested you in the text. This can be used as a conclusion for any literature answer.\n\nBuilding Block 5 - Key quotes: Select 5-6 short quotes (no more than one sentence each) from the text. Know who says them, in what context, and what they reveal about character or theme. Even paraphrasing a quote shows engagement with the original text.\n\nAdaptation strategy: When you see the exam question, identify which building blocks are relevant. A question on 'the most important character' uses blocks 1, 2, and 4. A question on 'a theme you found interesting' uses blocks 3, 2, and 4. A question on 'a scene that impressed you' uses blocks 2, 1, and 4. The personal response block (4) works as a conclusion for any question.\n\nPractise tip: write out your building blocks by hand multiple times in the weeks before the exam. Handwriting practice improves speed and reduces spelling errors under pressure. Time yourself writing a full literature answer in 20 minutes.",
